course: dictionary
lesson: 13281
title: Grabby and what it tells us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Informal words are used in everyday chat, not usually in school books. Grabby is one of them. Its meaning comes from the word grab, so you can start there. When you meet grabby, read the words around it. «Mia is grabby: she snatches every crayon before anyone else can ask.» Snatches and before anyone else tell you that grabby means wanting to take things for yourself.
explain: Grabby can also describe how someone touches others. «Lena said no when her cousin kept grabbing her arm, because she did not want to be touched.» Here grabby means grabbing or touching someone too much, and the word no shows that Lena did not like it. If someone is grabby with you when you do not want it, you can say no and tell a trusted adult.
explain: Grabby can describe ideas too. «The advert had a grabby headline that made everyone stop and read.» A grabby headline catches your attention quickly. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Grabby means wanting to grab or take things for yourself, often greedily.
fact: A grabby person touches or grabs others too much.
fact: A grabby headline or idea catches attention quickly.
fact: Grabby is an informal word, so you will hear it in chat more than in formal writing.
fact: Its forms are grabby, grabbier and grabbiest.
exercise: «The puppy was grabby, pawing at everyone's sandwiches.» What does grabby mean here? | Sleepy and calm | Very quiet and shy | *Wanting to grab food for itself | Lost in the park
exercise: «The newspaper had a grabby headline that made everyone read it.» What does grabby mean here? | Too heavy to lift | Made of thin paper | Short and boring | *Catching attention quickly
exercise: Sara says, «If someone is grabby with me and I don't want it, what should I do?» What is the best answer? | Keep it secret and hope it stops | Grab their arm back | Let them carry on because they are older | *Say no and tell a trusted adult
test: «Mia is grabby: she snatches every crayon before anyone else can ask.» What does grabby suggest about Mia? | *She wants to take things for herself | She is very tired and wants to sleep | She likes drawing more than anything | She is lost in the classroom
test: Which describes a grabby person? | *A person who touches and grabs others too much | A person who reads a book in the library | A person who sings quietly in the choir | A person who waits patiently in a queue
exam: Dad says, «Don't be grabby, let your sister have a turn.» What is Dad asking you to do? | Hide the toy under the bed | Grab the toy first before she can | Give the toy away to the dog | *Let your sister have a turn and stop taking everything
